WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.380
Take an invented population of 100 individuals, with a per capita growth rate of 0.2

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.880
per day. After ten days, you might expect 300.

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.960
If nothing stops it, there are about 739.

4
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.700
Growth compounds. Each new individual starts adding individuals of its own.

5
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:28.233
Call the number of individuals big N. Call the per capita rate little r.

6
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:33.053
Growth each day is little r times big N, so more individuals add more.

7
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.753
A constant little r gives a constant doubling time.

8
00:00:37.593 --> 00:00:43.633
Here it is about 3.47 days, whether the population holds 100 or 100,000.

9
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.567
Real populations meet a brake.

10
00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:52.367
Each individual typically gets less food, space or shelter, so births fall or deaths

11
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:57.567
rise. The logistic model puts the brake on the per capita rate, and assumes that

12
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:02.967
rate falls in a straight line, from little r when big N is near zero, to zero at big

13
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:06.027
K. Big K is the carrying capacity.

14
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.500
Total growth is that per capita rate times big N. At low big N, there are few

15
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.660
individuals to multiply.

16
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.880
Near big K, each one adds almost nothing.

17
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.960
So the product is greatest in the middle, at big K over 2.

18
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.680
There it is 50 individuals per day.

19
00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:27.426
Now run both models from 100 individuals.

20
00:01:28.406 --> 00:01:31.866
At day ten, the exponential model gives 739.

21
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.526
The logistic model gives about 451.

22
00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:38.266
They agree at first, then part.

23
00:01:39.146 --> 00:01:46.286
By day twenty, the exponential model predicts 5,460, and the logistic about 858.

24
00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:50.266
These numbers are illustrative, not measured for any species.

25
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.920
Big K is an equilibrium, not a ceiling.

26
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.100
If Big N starts above Big K, the model predicts decline back toward it.

27
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.680
If Big N starts below, the continuous model rises smoothly and never overshoots.

28
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.080
Real populations have age structure, time lags and a Big K that changes.

29
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.540
Any of these can produce overshoot.

30
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.640
Keep the rule: the per capita rate falls as Big N rises.
